The conjecture falsely assumes that avoiding induction requires abandoning the claim that science describes reality progressively. Popper was a critical realist who believed that surviving severe tests brings us closer to the truth (verisimilitude) without relying on inductive probability. By reducing the preference for corroborated theories to a mere 'language game' or 'scorecard,' the conjecture ignores Popper's explicit metaphysical commitment to truth as a regulative ideal, thereby attacking a straw man rather than his actual position.
