The analogy comparing scientific theory choice to a chess player's move fails because the constraints in chess are constitutive rules defined by humans, whereas the constraints in science (severe tests) are imposed by an independent reality. When a theory survives a severe test, it is not merely satisfying an internal community rule like a valid chess move; it has withstood an attempt by nature to falsify it. The conjecture's pragmatic framing erases this crucial asymmetry between human convention and empirical resistance, which is central to Popper's defense against irrationalism.
